Notebook[{
Cell["PadicAbs", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"4ad5d3f3-f509-4084-8af9-3e30d37769c3"],

Cell["\<\
PadicAbs[r,p] gives p^(-PadicOrder[r,p]) for an exact integer or rational r \
in integer base p>1; the value at zero is 0. PadicAbs[s] uses the base of an \
exact, lazy, or finite-precision p-adic object. A zero known only to O(p^a) \
has absolute-value bound Interval[{0,p^(-a)}]. Finite snapshots support only \
their stored base; lazy objects can be inspected at another prime base.\
\>", "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"9e9e51fe-5eb2-4754-9ed6-3c2ff8039fcf"],

Cell["\<\
Scalar inputs may use any integer base greater than one; a prime base gives \
the p-adic absolute value. Approximate objects support only their stored base.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"b71b9910-b4ac-4a46-bee8-c88f70194159"],

Cell["\<\
The absolute value of a finite-precision zero is bounded by its absolute \
precision and is returned as an Interval.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a133fc21-f131-417d-adec-d7e6ffeac3dd"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<PadicNumbers`\>\"", 
  "]"}]], "ExampleInitialization",ExpressionUUID->"8804c345-1d56-4c45-b2dd-\
c3e814314c91"],

Cell["Basic Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"c31c33c5-1bcb-482b-9812-af91a2176214"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

